From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 22:57:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 31/78] test(gastown): report the real file line in the
 pre_start bound check (gcp-oo0v)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Self-review catch on the guard added in the previous commit. The
unbounded-remote-call check stripped comments BEFORE numbering, so `grep -n`
counted lines in the stripped stream and the failure message pointed at a line
that does not exist in the file — mutation-testing the check reported line 83
for a call that lives at line 132.

Numbering now happens first and comments are dropped by matching the `NNN:#`
prefix, so the reported location is the file's own line. Comments still have to
be dropped: worktree-setup.sh's and polecat-worktree-reap.sh's headers describe
the unbounded fetch that caused gcp-ntbf/gcp-oo0v, and explaining the incident
must not read as committing it.

Behaviour is otherwise unchanged — the check catches exactly the same
mutations, verified by re-running all three (re-add the deacon pre_start, raise
a budget past setup_timeout, unwrap a single fetch) and confirming the last now
reports "132:" against a file whose own grep -n agrees.

A wrong line number in a guard's failure message is worth its own commit: this
test fires on someone else's pin bump months from now, and the first thing they
will do is open the file at the line it names.
---
 gastown/tests/test_agent_pre_start_budget.sh | 11 ++++++-----
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gastown/tests/test_agent_pre_start_budget.sh b/gastown/tests/test_agent_pre_start_budget.sh
index 770f070..b39ccb1 100755
--- a/gastown/tests/test_agent_pre_start_budget.sh
+++ b/gastown/tests/test_agent_pre_start_budget.sh
@@ -134,12 +134,13 @@ assert_bounded() {
         fail "$script must define budget_left and spend one shared wall clock, not bound each call independently"
 
     # And every call that can actually block for tens of seconds must go
-    # through it. Comments are stripped first: the headers explain the
-    # unbounded fetch that caused the incident, and saying so must not read as
-    # doing it.
+    # through it. Comments are dropped — the headers explain the unbounded
+    # fetch that caused the incident, and saying so must not read as doing it —
+    # but numbering happens FIRST, so the line this reports is the line in the
+    # file rather than an offset into the stripped stream.
     local unbounded
-    unbounded=$(grep -vE '^[[:space:]]*#' "$path" |
-        grep -nE '(^|[^-[:alnum:]_])git([[:space:]]|.*[[:space:]])(fetch|pull|push|clone|ls-remote)([[:space:]]|$)' |
+    unbounded=$(grep -nE '(^|[^-[:alnum:]_])git([[:space:]]|.*[[:space:]])(fetch|pull|push|clone|ls-remote)([[:space:]]|$)' "$path" |
+        grep -vE '^[0-9]+:[[:space:]]*#' |
         grep -v 'run_bounded' || true)
     [[ -z "$unbounded" ]] ||
         fail "$script calls a remote without a deadline: ${unbounded//$'\n'/ | }"
